### TITLE ###
HashCommentAtStatementPosition
### DESCRIPTION ###
A Python-style `#` comment at statement position gets the dedicated
RAD10023 with a suggestion pointing at `//`, instead of the generic
RAD10009 "Unexpected '# ...'".
### INPUT ###
# helper for X
print("hi")
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  (none)

# Symbol types
  (none)

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD10023 @ 1:1 - '#' does not start a comment here
    help: Rad comments use '//' ('#' only starts a comment in the shebang and inside args blocks)
### TITLE ###
HashCommentAfterArgsBlock
### DESCRIPTION ###
Regression for the exact shape from issue #148: `#` is valid arg-doc
syntax inside args blocks, which reinforces the habit; the same `#`
in the body must get the targeted error.
### INPUT ###
args:
    name str # the name to greet
# helper comment
print(name)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  (none)

# Symbol types
  (none)

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD10023 @ 3:1 - '#' does not start a comment here
    help: Rad comments use '//' ('#' only starts a comment in the shebang and inside args blocks)
### TITLE ###
ShebangIsNotAHashComment
### DESCRIPTION ###
Line-one shebang is grammar-handled and must produce no diagnostics.
### INPUT ###
#!/usr/bin/env rad
print("hi")
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  print @ 2:1 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  (none)

# Diagnostics
  (none)
### TITLE ###
TrailingHashCommentFallsBackToGeneric
### DESCRIPTION ###
When the ERROR node starts before the `#` (here at the expression
after `=`), the heuristic must not fire - we fall back to the generic
diagnostics rather than risk a wrong RAD10023 span.
### INPUT ###
x = 5 # trailing comment
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  x @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  comment @ 1:18 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  x (local): dynamic

# Diagnostics
  [error] RAD10009 @ 1:5 - Unexpected '5 # trailing'
  [error] RAD20028 @ 1:18 - Undefined identifier 'comment'
    help: did you mean 'count'?
